Title: ZODIAC WARS: BLOODLINES
Episode: 102, "THE HEARING"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-10
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 14 script pages of a 50-minute episode, plus a short tag. Season 1, gate ACOLYTE, the Libra episode. It follows episode 101 (short draft, not approved) by one day. Canon this draft respects: Ossara is in the Court's custody and said "I'll stand trial", and Adaeze answered "You'll be heard. By anyone who wants to." (the film); Adaeze's amendment, in her words in the film, is "No clause of this Accord stands unless the people it binds can read the reason for it"; the Sworn dissolved when she wrote it; Hera is a voice only, here from the Accord bell; Ossara's lines in the film that a number can be paid, and that at a certain scale a man says the cost aloud and the room stays quiet; the Schedule with its blank REASON column and the drop of red beside clause one are episode 101's. New in this draft, and the founder's to accept or strike: that the amendment, read against the blank Schedule, leaves every clause but nine without standing; that the clerks' first instinct is to draft reasons; and Ossara's distinction between a count made to close an account and a count that is carried. Also new: the Accord has four hundred and eleven clauses, and the text of clause one is "No House shall bear arms within the orbit of another." No new named character: the speakers at the hearing are unnamed. "Say the cost" is said once, by Ossara. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. THE BALANCE COURT — THE HALL OF THE SEATS — DAWN

The great circular hall under its glass dome, grey with first light. Twelve stone chairs in a ring. The pale thirteenth chair in the middle. Court ushers are carrying in plain wooden benches and setting them in rows between the Seats, where benches have never stood.

An OLD CLERK in a black gown, ink on his cuffs, chalks a notice on a slate by the great doors. We read it as he writes.

INSERT — THE SLATE: "OPEN HEARING. THE COURT AND VAHN OSSARA. ANYONE MAY SPEAK."

He steps back, reads it, and is not comfortable with the last three words. He raises the chalk to add something. High on the wall, the Accord bell hums, faintly, with nobody near it.

HERA (V.O.)
(from the bell; silver, exact, not unkind)
Leave it as she wrote it.

The Old Clerk lowers the chalk.

OLD CLERK
(to the bell, with a small bow)
There is no procedure for "anyone", Lady.

HERA (V.O.)
There was none for a thirteenth chair. It is still there.

The doors open. Morning comes in, and with it the sound of a very large crowd that has been waiting quietly since before light.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT, FIVE BRIGHT AND FOUR DARK. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

INT. THE HALL OF THE SEATS — MORNING

SUPER: "THE HEARING. DAY ONE."

Full. Every bench taken; people standing between the stone chairs, sitting on the plinths, lining the gallery. Dock workers, schoolchildren with a teacher, Court clerks in black, a delegation in Martian heat-stained jackets, three Neptunian wardens with blue fingertips. Nobody sits in the Seats.

At the long table: ADAEZE OKONKWO-REYES, the grey volume of the Schedule closed in front of her, the brass hand-bell at her elbow. Standing behind the last bench, against a pillar, in his work coat: KAEL MARREN. He has not slept.

In the middle of the ring, on a plain stool beside the thirteenth chair and not in it, sits VAHN OSSARA. No chains. A grey custody tunic. He looks like a man who has been reading the same forty thousand names every night.

Adaeze rings the hand-bell once.

ADAEZE
This is not a trial. A trial asks what a person did and what follows. We know what he did. He read it aloud himself. This is a hearing. It asks what the rest of us need to say to him, and what he has to say that we need to hear.
(she looks around the hall)
One at a time. No oath. If you lie, your neighbours will know.

A long silence. Then a DOCK WOMAN stands, broad, fifties, a shawl over her work clothes.

DOCK WOMAN
My brother was on your list.

OSSARA
(not looking away)
What was his name?

DOCK WOMAN
You tell me.

A terrible pause. Ossara closes his eyes.

OSSARA
What was his trade?

DOCK WOMAN
Crane rigger. Eastern quay.

OSSARA
(slowly; it is in him somewhere, in order)
Dorrin. Ansel Dorrin. He was the first name. I put him first because the page had to start somewhere.

The Dock Woman stands very still.

DOCK WOMAN
(quietly)
He'd have hated being first at anything.

She sits. A rustle goes through the hall; it is not anger. People are leaning forward.

A SCHOOLCHILD puts a hand up. The teacher tries to lower it. Adaeze nods at the child.

SCHOOLCHILD
Why did you write them down if you were going to do it anyway?

Ossara opens his mouth and finds he has no ready answer. He looks, for the first time, at Kael against his pillar.

OSSARA
(to the child)
That is the only good question I have been asked in twenty years. Let me come back to it.

INT. THE HALL OF THE SEATS — LATER

The light has moved along the floor. The Old Clerk is on his feet now, gown gathered, a paper in his hand. He does not address Ossara. He addresses the table.

OLD CLERK
Mediator. A matter of standing, before this goes further.

ADAEZE
(wary)
Go on.

OLD CLERK
In the vault, on the night of the bell, you amended the Accord. I have the text, in your hand, in coloured pencil.
(reading)
"No clause of this Accord stands unless the people it binds can read the reason for it."

ADAEZE
I know what I wrote.

OLD CLERK
Then you know what is in that book.

He points at the grey volume. The hall does not know what he means. Adaeze does. So does Kael.

ADAEZE
(carefully)
The Schedule has a reason beside clause nine.

OLD CLERK
And beside no other. I have kept that cabinet for thirty-one years, Mediator. I have never said it aloud, because it did not matter. It matters now. By your own sentence, the Accord is one clause long.

The hall takes this in. A murmur rises, and it is frightened.

MARS FOREMAN
(standing, from the heat-stained jackets)
So the water rule stands and nothing else? No borders? No tithe? No truce?

OLD CLERK
(quickly; he has a remedy and he loves it)
Only until the column is filled. The clerks' office can have draft reasons for all four hundred and eleven clauses by the end of the month. Sound reasons. We know what each clause is for.

High on the wall, the bell hums.

HERA (V.O.)
You know what each is used for.

The Old Clerk falters. Adaeze has gone pale; it is her own line from the vault, coming back.

ACT TWO

INT. THE HALL OF THE SEATS — CONTINUOUS

The murmur has become argument. Adaeze rings the hand-bell. It does not carry. She rings it again.

In the middle of the ring, Ossara stands up from his stool. He does not raise his voice. The hall quiets anyway, the way a room quiets for a man it has decided to hate carefully.

OSSARA
May the one being heard be heard?

ADAEZE
(a breath)
That is what it's for.

OSSARA
(to the Old Clerk, almost gently)
You are about to do what I did.

OLD CLERK
(stiff)
I am about to complete a record.

OSSARA
So was I.
(he turns, slowly, to the schoolchild)
You asked why I wrote them down. Here is the answer, and it is not a good one. I wrote them down so that it would be finished. Forty thousand names, in order, in a fair hand. When the last one was written I folded the paper and I felt something close, like a ledger at year's end. That is what a list can do. It can be a receipt.
(to the hall)
My master taught me to say the cost before the pour. She meant: say it and then stand beside it for the rest of your life. I heard: say it and then you have paid.

KAEL
(from the pillar; he did not mean to speak)
You said the room stays quiet.

OSSARA
(finding him)
At a certain scale. Yes. I thought that was the world's fault. That it could not hear a number that big. It was mine. The room was quiet because I had already left it.

He turns back to the Old Clerk.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
Four hundred and eleven reasons by the end of the month. Drafted by people who were not there, for the dead they never met, so that the column is full and the law stands and everyone may go home. I know that feeling exactly. It is the best I ever felt.

The Old Clerk says nothing. His paper has lowered an inch.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
(quietly, to the whole Court)
Say the cost.

It lands strangely, the foundry's oath in that mouth. Nobody answers. He answers himself.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
If you fill that column quickly, you will have a law again by winter. And you will have done to twelve thousand years of dead what I did to forty thousand living. You will have paid them.

Silence. Then the Dock Woman, not standing:

DOCK WOMAN
And if we fill it slow?

OSSARA
Then for a while you have one clause and each other. I am told that is how it started.

On the wall, the bell gives one soft, clear note: not a ring, a kind of assent.

ADAEZE
(low, to herself)
Hera.

HERA (V.O.)
I pressed this House toward balance for twelve thousand years. I took the scale for the thing weighed.
(a pause)
He is right. I dislike it very much.

INT. THE BALANCE COURT — A SIDE CLOISTER — DAY

A colonnade off the Hall, open to a small courtyard with one tree. The hearing has adjourned for an hour; its noise comes through the wall like weather. Adaeze walks fast. Kael keeps up.

ADAEZE
I need you to tell me he's wrong.

KAEL
He isn't.

ADAEZE
I know he isn't. I need someone to say it so I can argue.

She stops under the tree and turns on him, glasses in her fist.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
One clause. Twelve worlds. I did that with a pencil.

KAEL
You did it to stop the Sworn.

ADAEZE
I did it because it was true, which is worse. A true sentence doesn't care what it knocks down.
(she breathes)
Listen to me. The book has to be filled. Not fast. But filled, and by this Court, in law. Because you are nineteen and you will be dead in seventy years, and whoever you tell will be dead seventy after that, and I have just watched what happens to a thing that only people remember.

KAEL
(quietly)
It didn't happen to people. It happened to the book. The book had twelve thousand years and it kept one line.

ADAEZE
Because nobody made it keep more.

KAEL
Because you can't make paper carry anybody. Orla's name is on a bell. That's metal, it'll outlast both of us, and it carries nothing. Tamsin carries her. She scrubs the place where she fell every night. That's what carrying looks like. It has knees.

ADAEZE
And when Tamsin is gone?

Kael has no answer. She sees it, and does not press; she is too honest to win that way.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
(more gently)
I'm not saying the law carries them. I'm saying the law can make sure there is always someone whose job it is to. A person, named, for each. Replaced when they die. That is all a law has ever been, Kael. A way of making a promise outlive the people who made it.

KAEL
(slowly)
A law of the carried.

ADAEZE
I haven't drafted it. Don't name it yet.

KAEL
I'm still right about the paper.

ADAEZE
(putting her glasses back on)
You're right about the paper. I'm right about the seventy years. I suggest we both stay right and see who gets tired.

For the first time in two days, he almost smiles.

ACT THREE

INT. THE HALL OF THE SEATS — AFTERNOON

The benches full again. The light is long and gold through the dome. Adaeze stands at the table with the grey volume open in front of her. The Old Clerk sits apart, his paper folded away. Ossara is on his stool.

ADAEZE
The Court will not draft reasons.

A stir. She lets it pass.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
The amendment stands as written. Until a clause has a reason that the people it binds can read, it does not bind. It is kept, if it is kept, because we choose to keep it, day by day, in daylight. I recommend that we do. I can't order it.
(she turns the volume toward the hall)
This is the first page. Clause one: "No House shall bear arms within the orbit of another." The reason is blank. Last night something was added to it.

She holds the book up. Even from the gallery it can be seen: in the empty third column, one small dark-red mark.

MARS FOREMAN
What is it?

ADAEZE
I don't know whose it is. I know that it's one. And I know the Court has no idea how to find out who.

The hall is silent. Then, from the stool:

OSSARA
Mediator.

ADAEZE
Regent.

OSSARA
Not that. Not any more.
(he stands)
I have been heard. I am grateful, and I did not expect to be. I have a request, and you may refuse it.

ADAEZE
Say it.

OSSARA
I am the one person in this hall who knows precisely how a count goes bad. I know every turn of it. I could walk you through it blind.
(a breath)
Give me that one. The first name on the first clause. Not to write. I am not asking for a pencil. Let me be the one who goes and finds out who it was. And when I have found them, let me be the one who carries them. In custody. For as long as I last.

The hall erupts. The Dock Woman is on her feet; so is half the Martian delegation. The Old Clerk is shaking his head. Adaeze rings the bell and it does nothing.

Kael has come off his pillar. He is halfway down the aisle before he knows it, and he stops, because he does not know which side he is walking toward.

Adaeze looks at Ossara. Ossara looks at the red mark.

ADAEZE
(under the noise, to him alone)
Why?

OSSARA
Because I have forty thousand and one that I counted, and I have never carried a single one. I should like to learn on a stranger. I would not dare begin with Orla.

She stares at him. Above them, the Accord bell hangs silent. Hera says nothing at all, and it is the loudest thing in the room.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. THE BALANCE COURT — A CUSTODY ROOM — NIGHT

A plain room, more monk's cell than prison: a cot, a table, a window with no bars and a long drop. Ossara sits at the table. In front of him, the folded paper from the film, opened flat, forty thousand names in a fair hand.

He is not reading it. He has turned it over. The back is blank.

At the top of the blank side he has written, small, the number 1, and nothing after it.

In the corner of the room the air shimmers red. The UNBLED stands there, stooped, watching the page. A second gash on its arm has begun to shine wet.

OSSARA
(not turning)
I know. I saw.

The Unbled does not move.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
Does it hurt?

A sound like a great stone shifting. It might be yes.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
(very quietly)
Good.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 102 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
